-- NOTES

Merging

fast forward merge - the tip of the branch we are merging onto is the merge base
    - base branch now points to the last commit has of the branched branch

a - b - c (main) <mergebase>
         \
          d - e (feature)

git switch main; git merge feature;

a - b - c - d - e (main, feature)

------

Rebasing - as the name suggests, changing the merge base of  the target branch.
This means that the commit hash of every commit in the target branch is now going to change because every commit was created
with the mergebase in mind!

a - b - c <mergebase> - f - g - h(main)
         \
          d - e (feature)

git switch feature; git rebase main
a - b - c - f - g - h (main) <new mergebase> - d - e (feature)
    You can now perform a fastforward merge (Important!, feature branch still hasn't been merged into main)

Rebasing a public branch is bad! (Rebase your branch on top of a public branch)
{
    What would happen if you did (!! Do not do this !!)
        git switch main; git rebase feature

    a - b - c (main)
            \
            d - e (feature)
    a - b - c - d - e (main, feature)

    a - b - c - d - e - f - h (main)<mergecommit>
                     \     /
                       g(feature)
}

Also is there a fastforward rebase. (current feature branch is upto date.)

